// @check
// @error-at 30:19 argument 1: operator '+' is not defined for {foo: string} and {foo: string} (the body of `double` uses it on both sides of '+')
// @error-at 31:16 operator '+' is not defined for i32 and string (the body of `add` applies '+' to arguments 1 and 2)
// @error-at 32:17 argument 1: operator '+' is not defined for {foo: string} and i32 (the body of `bump` applies '+' to this argument)
// @error-at 33:50 operator '+' is not defined for {foo: string} and {foo: string} (the call's argument types)
// CONTRACT (must-error): a deferred binary-op refusal names the ARGUMENT it is
// about and anchors THERE — the reported defect was `double({ foo: "bar" })` on
// `(a) => a + a` reporting `… for {foo: string} and {foo: string} (the call's
// argument types)` with the caret on the call's closing paren, for a call with
// ONE argument. Two things were wrong and both are pinned here.
//
// THE ANCHOR is the argument expression, at its START token — the call node's
// own anchor is its LAST token, so it sat a whole call away from the argument
// the sentence is about, and an argument that is a composite would otherwise
// caret its own closing brace.
//
// THE ATTRIBUTION distinguishes the three shapes. One argument on BOTH sides
// says so ("uses it on both sides"), so two identical operand types can no
// longer be read as two arguments. TWO arguments names both and takes no
// `argument N:` head, because a head would blame one of them. One operand
// CONCRETE names the single argument. The refusal sentence itself is the direct
// spelling's, verbatim, so the two stay greppable as one rule.
//
// The fourth line is the UNATTRIBUTED fallback, kept: `xs[0] + xs[1]` reaches
// the hole through a SHAPE, so no argument's own type is the operand and there
// is nothing to point at.
const double = (a) => a + a
const add = (a, b) => a + b
const bump = (a) => a + 1
function heads(xs) { return xs[0] + xs[1] }
const _r1 = double({ foo: "bar" })
const _r2 = add(1, "x")
const _r3 = bump({ foo: "bar" })
const _r4 = heads([{ foo: "bar" }, { foo: "baz" }])
